\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A prime $p$ is a twin member if at least one of $p-2$ and $p+2$ is prime. The two chosen summands need not come from the same twin pair and need not be distinct.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every even integer $N>4208$, do there exist primes $p,q$ with $N=p+q$ such that each of $p$ and $q$ is a twin member? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.02381}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every even integer above four thousand two hundred eight be expressed as a sum of two primes, each belonging to some twin-prime pair?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 9400120 (AMR-093-0120). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Tushar Pandey, \emph{An intermediate conjecture between Goldbach and Dubner: every even number is the sum of a prime and a twin prime}, arXiv:2608.02381v2 (2026) Introduction, p. 1, definition of twin members and Dubner conjecture. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.02381}.
\item[S3] Wikipedia contributors, \emph{List of unsolved problems in mathematics}, revision 1366636767 (2026) Number theory, Dubner's conjecture. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}.
